// Practice quiz: Lambdas and functional interfaces in depth
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: collections-streams/lambdas-and-streams/lambdas-and-functional-interfaces-in-depth

::o1 consumer::Which standard functional interface fits a rule that receives an order, writes it to a log and returns nothing? {
=Consumer#Correct. A Consumer accepts one value and returns nothing.
~Supplier#Not quite. A Supplier takes no argument and produces a value.
~Function#Not quite. A Function returns a result; this rule returns nothing.
~Predicate#Not quite. A Predicate returns true or false.
}

::o1 andThen order::Function f adds 1 to an Integer and Function g doubles it. What does f.andThen(g).apply(3) return? {
=8#Correct. andThen runs f first, giving 4, and then g doubles it.
~7#Not quite. That runs g first and then f, which is f.compose(g).
~6#Not quite. That applies only g; andThen runs both functions.
~4#Not quite. That applies only f; andThen passes its result on to g.
}

::o1 and negate::Predicate isEmpty tests s.isEmpty() and Predicate isLong tests whether s has more than 5 characters. Which predicate is true only for non-empty Strings of at most 5 characters? {
=isEmpty.negate().and(isLong.negate())#Correct. Each rule is reversed on its own, and and requires both reversed rules to hold.
~isEmpty.and(isLong).negate()#Not quite. negate reverses the whole and, which is never true, so this is true for every String, even the empty one.
~isEmpty.negate().and(isLong)#Not quite. This keeps only the long Strings.
~isLong.negate()#Not quite. The empty String also has at most 5 characters, so it passes.
}

::o2 functional interface::Which type can be the target of a lambda? {
=An interface with one abstract method and two default methods#Correct. Default methods do not count, so exactly one abstract method remains.
~An interface with two abstract methods#Not quite. A lambda can supply only one method body.
~An interface with only default and static methods#Not quite. With no abstract method there is nothing for the lambda to implement.
~An abstract class with one abstract method#Not quite. A lambda targets only a functional interface, never a class.
}

::o2 annotation::What does adding @FunctionalInterface to an interface do? {
=The compiler reports an error unless the interface has exactly one abstract method#Correct. The annotation is an optional compile-time check of the functional-interface rule.
~It is required before any lambda can target the interface#Not quite. It is optional; any interface with one abstract method can be a lambda target.
~It checks the rule when the program runs#Not quite. The check happens at compile time.
~It turns every method of the interface into a default method#Not quite. It changes no methods; it only asks the compiler to check the rule.
}

::o3 reassigned local::A method declares int count \= 0;, creates a Runnable lambda whose body prints count, and later runs count++;. What happens? {
=A compile error, because count is not effectively final#Correct. A captured local must never be reassigned, and count++ reassigns it.
~It compiles and prints 0#Not quite. Java rejects the lambda before the program can run.
~It compiles and prints 1#Not quite. Java rejects the lambda at compile time, so nothing prints.
~It throws an exception when the lambda runs#Not quite. The rule is checked by the compiler, not at run time.
}

::o3 fix captured local::In that same method, which change lets the lambda print the value count had when the lambda was created? {
=Copy count into a new local, int snapshot \= count;, that is never reassigned, and print snapshot#Correct. snapshot is effectively final, so the lambda may capture it.
~Declare count final and keep count++#Not quite. A final variable cannot be incremented, so this is still a compile error.
~Move count++ above the lambda#Not quite. count is still reassigned, so it is still not effectively final.
~Declare count volatile#Not quite. volatile is not allowed on a local variable.
}
